WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.219
Fire in space doesn't behave like fire on Earth.

2
00:00:04.219 --> 00:00:06.858
Without gravity, flames don't rise.

3
00:00:06.858 --> 00:00:09.496
They burn as slow, glowing spheres,

4
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:13.794
and understanding that is key to keeping astronauts safe.

5
00:00:13.794 --> 00:00:19.147
This is AndroFlame, our project for the 2026 NASA Space Apps Challenge:

6
00:00:19.147 --> 00:00:20.504
Flame in Freefall.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.461
Everything starts on the AndroFlame tab.

8
00:00:24.461 --> 00:00:29.897
Our mission is to take fifty years of NASA microgravity combustion research,

9
00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:34.261
twenty-four investigations and nearly two thousand test runs,

10
00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:37.122
and turn it into answers anyone can use.

11
00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:41.414
This page also explains who the platform is for, its limits,

12
00:00:41.414 --> 00:00:43.488
and which tools to try first.

13
00:00:44.438 --> 00:00:45.578
New to space fire?

14
00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.605
Open the New in Space Fire menu.

15
00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:51.658
The Learning FireLab puts you in the shoes of a NASA researcher.

16
00:00:51.658 --> 00:00:56.598
In the FLEX droplet lab, you'll see why a flame on Earth is a rising teardrop,

17
00:00:56.598 --> 00:00:59.512
but in orbit it becomes a near-perfect sphere.

18
00:00:59.512 --> 00:01:04.388
You watch the flame, change a condition, and measure the droplet as it burns.

19
00:01:04.388 --> 00:01:09.455
A second lab, BASS two, lets you design a solid fuel experiment and compare your

20
00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:12.875
results with real flight tests from the space station.

21
00:01:13.825 --> 00:01:19.049
The Fire Dynamics Explorer teaches the basics with an interactive 3D fire

22
00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:23.200
tetrahedron: fuel, heat, oxidizer, and the chain reaction.

23
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.062
Pick a face, or follow a guided pathway,

24
00:01:26.062 --> 00:01:30.785
and it links you straight to the NASA experiments that studied it.

25
00:01:31.735 --> 00:01:34.098
To dig deeper, open the Apps menu.

26
00:01:34.098 --> 00:01:39.727
The Catalog Explorer searches all twenty-four investigations by material, oxygen,

27
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:41.256
pressure, and airflow.

28
00:01:41.256 --> 00:01:46.746
Tick a few studies, and Compare Studies lines them up side by side: conditions,

29
00:01:46.746 --> 00:01:50.915
hardware, fuels, and measurements, with gaps clearly marked.

30
00:01:50.915 --> 00:01:53.903
The Coverage Matrix answers a key question:

31
00:01:53.903 --> 00:01:56.891
has anyone already tested these conditions?

32
00:01:56.891 --> 00:02:01.686
Enter a lunar habitat atmosphere, and it shows which test rows match,

33
00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:03.285
and where the gaps are.

34
00:02:03.285 --> 00:02:08.913
Scenario Ranking then scores each study for the Moon, Mars, or the space station.

35
00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:13.445
The Ask tab lets you question the evidence directly.

36
00:02:13.445 --> 00:02:16.959
Every answer is computed from the actual NASA data,

37
00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:21.712
with numbered citations that point back to the exact table row, page,

38
00:02:21.712 --> 00:02:23.021
or video timestamp.

39
00:02:23.021 --> 00:02:28.807
And if the records can't answer a question, the system says so, instead of guessing.

40
00:02:29.757 --> 00:02:32.213
Finally, on every page, there's Dr.

41
00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:34.037
Spark, a friendly chatbot.

42
00:02:34.037 --> 00:02:39.159
Ask it anything, from simple fire science to where a material was tested,

43
00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:44.773
and it answers with sources, then suggests pathways to the right tool inside the

44
00:02:44.773 --> 00:02:45.053
app.

45
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.017
AndroFlame turns decades of NASA flight data into clear, cited,

46
00:02:51.017 --> 00:02:56.897
and explorable knowledge, helping the next generation of explorers keep fire under

47
00:02:56.897 --> 00:02:58.617
control, beyond gravity.
